## Why This Is Asked

Tests advanced fairness and isolation in a multi-tenant KCNA cluster; demonstrates practical reliability under bursty traffic.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant rate limiting (token bucket, leaky bucket)
- Global throughput vs. per-tenant isolation
- Distributed state store for buckets (Redis, etc.)
- Backpressure mechanisms and producer signaling
- Canary strategy and measurable success criteria

## Code Example

```javascript
// simplified token bucket sketch
class TokenBucket {
  constructor(rate, capacity) {
    this.rate = rate;
    this.capacity = capacity;
    this.tokens = capacity;
    this.lastRefill = Date.now();
  }

  allow(n = 1) {
    this.refill();
    if (this.tokens >= n) {
      this.tokens -= n;
      return true;
    }
    return false;
  }

  refill() {
    const now = Date.now();
    const elapsed = (now - this.lastRefill) / 1000;
    this.tokens = Math.min(this.capacity, this.tokens + elapsed * this.rate);
    this.lastRefill = now;
  }
}

// Global limiter wrapping per-tenant buckets
class TenantRateController {
  constructor(tenants, globalRate) {
    this.buckets = new Map();
    this.globalLimiter = new TokenBucket(globalRate, globalRate * 2);
    for (const [id, quota] of tenants) {
      this.buckets.set(id, new TokenBucket(quota.rate, quota.burst));
    }
  }

  tryProcess(tenantId, n = 1) {
    const bucket = this.buckets.get(tenantId);
    if (!bucket || !bucket.allow(n)) return false;
    if (!this.globalLimiter.allow(n)) {
      bucket.tokens += n; // rollback tenant token
      return false;
    }
    return true;
  }
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
This question tests the ability to design a time-aware, multi-sink Kafka workflow with correct offset handling and idempotence when routing late-arriving data.

## Key Concepts
- EventTime versus wall-clock time
- Late-arrival detection with separate sink routing
- Global idempotence across multiple sinks
- Offset commit semantics in multi-path processing
- Canary test design for on-time versus late events

## Code Example
```javascript
function onMessage(msg) {
  if (isLate(msg.eventTime)) {
    routeToSink('late_user_events', msg)
  } else {
    routeToSink('analytics', msg)
  }
  commitOffsets([msg.offset])
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Assesses practical knowledge of multi-tenant KCNA scaling, ordering guarantees, and zero-downtime rebalancing.

## Key Concepts
- Per-tenant partition ownership and fast-rebalancing
- Two-phase commit style fencing and graceful stop
- Ordering guarantees and exactly-once semantics across transfers
- Observability, canaries, rollback

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo protocol for per-tenant partition reallocation
async function rebalanceTenantPartitions(tenantId, partitions, targetBrokers){
  await freezeOffsets(tenantId, partitions);
  await migrateData(partitions, targetBrokers);
  await commitOwnershipChange(tenantId, partitions, targetBrokers);
  await resumeProcessing(tenantId);
}
```

