## Why This Is Asked

Tests understanding of access control in KCNA, multi-tenant isolation, and auditable, low-friction token rotation.

## Key Concepts

- Tenant-scoped ACLs and per-topic allowlists
- Token-based authentication with runtime rotation
- Non-blocking authorization checks and audit logs
- Backward-compatible ACL evolution

## Code Example

```javascript
// ACL data model and quick middleware example
const acl = {
  "tenantA": { topics: ["t1","t2"], actions: ["publish","subscribe"], version: 1 }
}
function authorize(tenant, action, topic, tokenVersion) {
  const entry = acl[tenant];
  if (!entry) return false;
  return entry.version === tokenVersion && entry.topics.includes(topic) && entry.actions.includes(action);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you store ACLs to support auditability and rotation without downtime?
- How would you test for token revocation affecting in-flight requests?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests the ability to design robust per-topic schema validation with proper error handling and reliable offset management in Kafka consumer applications.

## Key Concepts
- JSON schema validation with type checking for required fields
- Per-topic dead-letter queue (DLQ) implementation
- Offset commit semantics with at-least-once delivery guarantees
- Idempotent message publishing and canary testing strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
function validateUserEvent(rawMessage) {
  try {
    const payload = JSON.parse(rawMessage);
    if (typeof payload.userId === 'string' && 
        typeof payload.signupDate === 'string' && 
        typeof payload.metadata === 'object') {
      return { valid: true, payload };
    }
  } catch (error) {
    // Parsing error
  }
  return { valid: false, error: 'Invalid user event schema' };
}
```

## Testing Approach
Deploy canary with mixed valid/invalid messages to validate schema enforcement, DLQ routing, and offset commit behavior before full rollout.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to design a lightweight idempotent sink that works across multiple KCNA topics, focusing on dedup without heavy infrastructure.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotent sinks
- Per-message deduplication
- In-memory TTL caches
- At-least-once guarantees across topics

## Code Example
```javascript
class DedupeStore {
  constructor(ttlMs) { this.ttl = ttlMs; this.map = new Map(); }
  seen(id) { const t = this.map.get(id); return t && t > Date.now(); }
  mark(id) { this.map.set(id, Date.now() + this.ttl); }
  evict() { /* naive cleanup */ }
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle memory pressure or restarts?
- How to test TTL eviction and cross-topic ordering?

## Why This Is Asked

Cross-region locality is real; this tests ordering, fault tolerance, and cross-region controls with per-tenant isolation.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant locality constraints
- Vector clocks for per-tenant ordering
- Commit barriers and selective replication
- Idempotent apply and deduplication

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo per-tenant apply with idempotence
function applyEvent(tenantId, event, state) {
  const last = state[tenantId]?.seq ?? 0;
  if (event.seq <= last) return state;
  state[tenantId] = { seq: event.seq, data: event.data };
  return state;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate correctness under regional outages?
- How would you adapt if tenants have variable data retention requirements?
